// U5 L5 Exploitation Prevention — 30-question bank

::U5L5 Q01::Exploitation means {=taking unfair advantage of another person for someone else's benefit ~respecting a boundary ~ordinary disagreement ~professional counseling}

::U5L5 Q02::Human trafficking always requires kidnapping. {FALSE}

::U5L5 Q03::Force involves {=physical compulsion or threatened violence ~a verified job description ~respect for privacy ~trusted support}

::U5L5 Q04::Fraud involves {=deception ~emergency care ~a clear boundary ~accurate reporting}

::U5L5 Q05::Coercion may involve {=threats, intimidation, pressure, or manipulation ~voluntary agreement ~transparent expectations ~independent verification}

::U5L5 Q06::A single warning sign {=raises a possible concern but does not prove trafficking ~proves a legal conclusion ~requires confrontation ~should be ignored}

::U5L5 Q07::Which pattern is most concerning? {=secrecy, isolation, control, and threats escalating together ~a verified employer answering questions ~a friend respecting a boundary ~a transparent school activity}

::U5L5 Q08::A suspicious contact asks for location and secrecy. The safest first response is to {=stop sharing information, disengage, and involve trusted support ~continue to gather proof ~meet the contact ~send identification}

::U5L5 Q09::Personal security includes {=protecting identity, location, communication, and access to support ~sharing passwords with new contacts ~meeting secretly ~ignoring boundaries}

::U5L5 Q10::Grooming is best described as {=gradual trust-building or boundary testing for later exploitation ~a medical screening ~an emergency service ~a transparent job interview}

::U5L5 Q11::A gift gives another person the right to control behavior. {FALSE}

::U5L5 Q12::Which job-offer feature is a red flag? {=The business cannot be verified and demands secrecy ~The duties and employer are transparent ~A trusted adult helps verify it ~Questions are welcomed}

::U5L5 Q13::Why should a student not personally investigate possible trafficking? {=Continuing contact or confrontation can increase danger ~Students must collect legal evidence ~Investigation proves courage ~Trusted help is unnecessary}

::U5L5 Q14::If a friend discloses fear and control, a supportive response is to {=listen, avoid blame, and connect to trusted help ~promise secrecy in all circumstances ~confront the person involved ~question why the friend did not leave}

::U5L5 Q15::Why might leaving exploitation be difficult? {=fear, threats, dependence, isolation, or control may create barriers ~victims prefer harm ~gifts create consent ~warning signs are always imaginary}

::U5L5 Q16::Which statement is victim blaming? {="They accepted a gift, so the exploitation is their fault." ~"The exploiter is responsible for exploitation." ~"Safety support may be needed." ~"Leaving can be complex."}

::U5L5 Q17::Online recruitment risk may involve {=secrecy, personal-information requests, unrealistic promises, and boundary pressure ~only strangers and kidnapping ~verified transparent communication only ~no use of social media}

::U5L5 Q18::Substance use can increase vulnerability because it may {=impair judgment or be used in exploitative contexts ~prove trafficking ~make victims responsible ~eliminate coercion}

::U5L5 Q19::A contact becomes angry when a student asks to verify an opportunity. This evidence supports {=concern and a need to disengage or seek help ~a definite legal diagnosis ~continued private contact ~sharing more information}

::U5L5 Q20::When a direct threat creates immediate danger, the correct priority is {=emergency assistance and safety ~a routine TOR message before emergency help ~personal investigation ~a future appointment only}

::U5L5 Q21::Which response maintains a boundary? {="I am not sharing that information. I am ending this contact." ~"I will send my location to prove trust." ~"I will meet secretly." ~"I will keep talking to collect evidence."}

::U5L5 Q22::Trafficking can affect {=people of different genders, backgrounds, and communities ~only one gender ~only strangers in cities ~only people from one income group}

::U5L5 Q23::Controlling transportation and communication are examples of {=control and isolation concerns ~transparent support ~ordinary verification ~medical treatment}

::U5L5 Q24::Debt manipulation means {=using a claimed debt to control or exploit ~paying an ordinary bill transparently ~seeking healthcare ~setting a boundary}

::U5L5 Q25::Which conclusion uses evidence rather than stereotype? {=The secrecy, threats, and isolation raise concern and require a safety response ~The person's background proves trafficking ~Only strangers are dangerous ~One sign proves everything}

::U5L5 Q26::A friend suggests messaging a threatening person to learn more. The safest reply is {=Do not investigate; involve trusted support and emergency help if danger is immediate ~Continue anonymously ~Arrange a meeting ~Make an accusation publicly}

::U5L5 Q27::Reporting means {=sharing a concern with an appropriate responsible resource ~posting private details publicly ~confronting a suspected exploiter ~guaranteeing a legal conclusion}

::U5L5 Q28::Which sequence best matches the lesson? {=Recognize, protect safety, disengage, seek trusted help, escalate appropriately ~Investigate, confront, meet, accuse ~Ignore, isolate, conceal, delay ~Share, travel, comply, keep secret}

::U5L5 Q29::A person may know or initially trust someone involved in exploitation. {TRUE}

::U5L5 Q30::Strong critical analysis of a suspicious timeline should {=identify evidence and escalation, limit conclusions, and select a safety-first response ~determine guilt from one clue ~recommend continued contact ~blame the person at risk}
